341 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 82 0 R
/T (With exception-based validation, each valid line follows the normal parse path and exceptions are raised only for invalid rows. When bad rows are rare, this is usually efficient and keeps validation code focused. With if-statements, each line is checked explicitly before fields are used; this avoids exception overhead and is often preferable when malformed rows are common. Both approaches are O\(1\) work per line for a fixed number of fields, so total processing is O\(n\) for n lines. Their practical constant costs differ; benchmark with representative data to compare.)
/E (With exception-based validation, each valid line follows the normal parse path and exceptions are raised only for invalid rows. When bad rows are rare, this is usually efficient and keeps validation code focused. With if-statements, each line is checked explicitly before fields are used; this avoids exception overhead and is often preferable when malformed rows are common. Both approaches are O\(1\) work per line for a fixed number of fields, so total processing is O\(n\) for n lines. Their practical constant costs differ; benchmark with representative data to compare.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [16]
>>
endobj
